\

\f0\b 1. The Basics of Connectivity \

\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Switches:
\f1\b0 \'a0Used to connect multiple devices (like computers) on a network via ethernet cables. They manage how data (electrical signals) is sent between devices.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Physical Ports:
\f1\b0 \'a0Switches come in various sizes (e.g., 8-port, 48-port) to accommodate different network scales.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 2. Hubs vs. Switches \

\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Hubs (The "Dumb" Predecessor):
\f1\b0 \'a0When a hub receives data, it broadcasts the signal to\'a0
\f0\b every port/device
\f1\b0 \'a0on                                                                                                                                                            the network, regardless of the intended recipient. This creates security risks and traffic inefficiency.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Switches (The "Smart" Successor):
\f1\b0 \'a0A switch selectively forwards data only to the specific device that needs it, significantly improving network efficiency and security.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 3. How a Switch Works \

\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 MAC Addresses:
\f1\b0 \'a0Every device has a unique, permanent identifier called a\'a0
\f5\i MAC address
\f1\i0 \'a0(Layer 2 address).\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 CAM Table:
\f1\b0 \'a0Switches maintain a\'a0
\f5\i Content Addressable Memory
\f1\i0 \'a0(CAM) table. They learn which MAC address is connected                                                                                                                                        to which specific port and store this information to make intelligent forwarding decisions.\
\ls6\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Layering:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl1
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Layer 1 (Physical):
\f1\b0 \'a0The electrical signals/wires.\
\ls6\ilvl1
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Layer 2 (Data Link):
\f1\b0 \'a0The switch level, using MAC addresses and\'a0
\f5\i frames
\f1\i0 \'a0to move data.\
\ls6\ilvl1
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Layer 3 (Network):
\f1\b0 \'a0IP addresses and\'a0
\f5\i packets
\f1\i0 \'a0(switches typically don't process these, though they are involved in communication).\
\ls6\ilvl1
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 4. Cisco CLI Command \

\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 The first command learned is:\'a0show mac-address-table. This allows you to view the switch's brain (CAM table) to see mappings of MAC addresses to ports.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 5. Wireless Networks \

\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f3\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 Wireless Access Points, while modern and essential, often function more like\'a0
\f0\b hubs
\f1\b0 \'a0at the base level, broadcasting traffic,                                                                                                                                                    which is why hardwired ethernet connections are generally preferred for stability and security.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
